# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever cloned the repository.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites every text
# file to CRLF on checkout. Nothing about that is visible until a check runs:
# Prettier's `endOfLine` defaults to "lf" and `ruff format` normalises to LF
# too, so `npm run lint` fails on every file in the tree at once, reporting a
# formatting problem in code the contributor has not touched. The honest fix is
# to stop the rewrite rather than to relax both formatters, because the
# formatters are right — this repository is LF and the published bundle is LF.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: decide text-vs-binary by content, and check text
# out as LF on every platform. The working tree then matches the index, which
# is what makes a Windows clone lint clean.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, stated rather than sniffed. The font files are the ones that matter:
# each is its publisher's own build byte for byte, and `docs/legal.md` §"Bundled
# fonts" turns on that being true — OFL 1.1 condition 3 forbids a Reserved Font
# Name on a modified version, and a newline conversion is a modification. A
# corrupted woff2 also fails silently, as a page that renders in a fallback
# face rather than as an error.
*.woff2 binary
*.woff   binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.xlsx   binary
*.xls    binary

# Committed data. LF, and diffed as text — `data/published/` is reviewed by
# reading its diff, which is the whole point of committing it.
*.json text eol=lf

# The vendored fonts and the licence text beside them: left exactly as their
# publisher shipped them, newlines included.
#
# `site/public/fonts/ibm-plex/OFL.txt` is committed CRLF because that is how IBM
# ship it, and the rule above would rewrite it on the next checkout. The
# rewrite would not breach the OFL — condition 3's Reserved Font Name applies to
# a Modified Version of the font, not to the licence file — but `docs/legal.md`
# §"Bundled fonts" rests on every file in these directories being the
# publisher's own, verified by `cmp` against the npm package, and a normalised
# copy is one that no longer compares equal. The check that proves the claim is
# worth more than consistent newlines in a file nobody edits.
site/public/fonts/** -text
